<header><p class="st-kicker">county · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">County of Armagnac</h1><p class="st-meta">960 CE – 1497 CE (approx.) · capital Lectoure</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A Gascon county whose counts gave their name to a faction in France's civil war and fought Louis XI to the bitter end.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Armagnac was carved out of the duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gascony">Gascony</a> around 960 for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-i-of-armagnac">Bernard the Squinter</a>, a younger son of the Gascon ducal house. His descendants ruled the hill country around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/auch">Auch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lectoure">Lectoure</a> and gradually gathered neighbouring counties such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fezensac">Fezensac</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rodez">Rodez</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The counts were among the greatest lords of the south-west, shifting between the English in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/duchy-of-aquitaine">Guyenne</a> and the French crown. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernard-vii-count-of-armagnac">Bernard VII</a> became constable of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and led the Armagnac party against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgundians">Burgundians</a> in the civil war of the 1410s until his murder in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> in 1418. The land was known for wine and its brandy, later named after it.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Count John V defied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a> and was killed when royal troops took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lectoure">Lectoure</a> in 1473. The last count, Charles I, died in 1497, and the county passed to the duke of Alençon and later to the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-albret">Albret</a> before returning to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/kingdom-of-france">Kingdom of France</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="county, 960 CE – 1497 CE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M20 74 H80 V60 H20 Z"/><path d="M20 60 Q35 46 50 60 Q65 46 80 60"/><circle cx="20" cy="54" r="3"/><circle cx="35" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="50" cy="44" r="3"/><circle cx="65" cy="48" r="3"/><circle cx="80" cy="54" r="3"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">county</p><p class="st-emblem-region">France and the Low Countries</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:78.81%;width:10.69%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>960 CE – 1497 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>county</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Lectoure</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Bernard the Squinter, Bernard VII of Armagnac, John V of Armagnac</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gascon</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd>France</dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Armagnac</dd></dl></aside></div>
